$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The litigation the settlement ended</h2>
<p>The parties married on 31 January 2013; a son was born in 2017. The wife's complaint under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 (Complaint No. 989 of 2020) led, on 12 February 2025, to a trial court order of Rs 1.5 lakh per month covering maintenance for the child and alternative accommodation. The husband applied under Section 25(2) to alter that order; an advocate commissioner's inquiry followed, which the First Appellate Court set aside in December 2025. The Calcutta High Court, in C.R.R. No. 553 of 2026, treated that challenge as infructuous while leaving the accommodation charges open — and the husband carried the matter to the Supreme Court by special leave.</p>

<h2>From appeal to settlement</h2>
<p>Before the Bench of Justice Nongmeikapam Kotiswar Singh and Justice Satish Chandra Sharma, the case changed character. Rather than adjudicate another slice of the dispute, the Court directed the parties to explore settlement. They returned with comprehensive terms, which the judgment records and makes part of the order.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Rs 30 crore</h3><p>Permanent alimony to the wife in full and final settlement of her claims.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Rs 45 crore</h3><p>Held in trust for the child, securing his maintenance, education and future.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Residence</h3><p>A defined residence right for the wife, subject to recorded conditions.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>14 cases</h3><p>The listed proceedings between the parties and their families brought to a close.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The settlement also preserved a parenting plan for the child, with a good-faith consultation mechanism for future disagreements — a detail worth noting, because financial settlements that ignore parenting questions tend to generate the next round of litigation.</p>

<h2>Article 142: the power to finish the matter</h2>
<p>Two further steps gave the settlement finality. First, invoking Article 142 of the Constitution, the Court gave quietus to all the disputes covered by the terms. Second — and decisively — it dissolved the marriage itself under Article 142 and directed that a decree be drawn, without remitting the parties to a mutual-consent petition before the Family Court. The appeal and all pending applications were disposed of in terms of the settlement.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The DV Act proceeding that began this litigation was designed to secure interim protection, not to end a marriage. But once spouses reach genuine terms, Article 142 lets the Supreme Court do what no single forum below could: dissolve the marriage, settle the money, fix the parenting arrangement and close every connected case in one order.</p>
</div>

<h2>What this means for litigants</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>For parties locked in multi-forum matrimonial conflict:</strong> the lesson is structural. Interim orders — maintenance, accommodation charges, commissioner inquiries — are pressure points, not endpoints. A settlement negotiated when the matter reaches a court that can bind everything at once is worth far more than piecemeal victories below. Listing every pending case in the settlement schedule, securing the child's provision separately from the spouse's, and recording the terms in the court's order are what convert a truce into a conclusion.</p>
</div>

<p>Few families litigate at these figures, but the architecture of the order — alimony, a child's trust, a residence right, a parenting plan and a schedule of closed cases — is a template that scales to any matrimonial settlement, whatever the amounts involved.</p>
